WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.606
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.906 --> 00:00:08.886
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.186 --> 00:00:16.598
Unit three: Language as Power. In this unit, you will analyse how language shapes power and perception.

4
00:00:17.583 --> 00:00:19.420
linguistic imperialism.

5
00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:26.114
linguistic imperialism means the dominance of one language over others, often linked to power.

6
00:00:26.414 --> 00:00:31.211
Some scholars describe the global spread of English as linguistic imperialism.

7
00:00:31.911 --> 00:00:36.143
Your turn. Say: linguistic imperialism.

8
00:00:38.843 --> 00:00:40.587
linguistic imperialism.

9
00:00:41.583 --> 00:00:42.712
loaded term.

10
00:00:43.412 --> 00:00:48.848
loaded term means a word carrying strong emotional or political implications.

11
00:00:49.148 --> 00:00:52.158
'Regime' is a loaded term compared with 'government'.

12
00:00:52.858 --> 00:00:56.091
Your turn. Say: loaded term.

13
00:00:58.791 --> 00:00:59.966
loaded term.

14
00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:02.346
prestige dialect.

15
00:01:03.046 --> 00:01:09.062
prestige dialect means the variety of a language associated with education and status.

16
00:01:09.362 --> 00:01:12.999
The Phnom Penh accent is often treated as a prestige dialect.

17
00:01:13.699 --> 00:01:17.501
Your turn. Say: prestige dialect.

18
00:01:20.201 --> 00:01:21.597
prestige dialect.

19
00:01:22.583 --> 00:01:23.735
code of power.

20
00:01:24.435 --> 00:01:29.221
code of power means the language and behaviour rules expected by those in power.

21
00:01:29.521 --> 00:01:33.657
Students from rural areas must learn the code of power.

22
00:01:34.357 --> 00:01:37.195
Your turn. Say: code of power.

23
00:01:39.895 --> 00:01:41.012
code of power.

24
00:01:42.017 --> 00:01:43.006
euphemism.

25
00:01:43.706 --> 00:01:47.842
euphemism means a mild word used instead of a harsh or direct one.

26
00:01:48.142 --> 00:01:50.374
'Let go' is a euphemism for 'fired'.

27
00:01:51.074 --> 00:01:53.947
Your turn. Say: euphemism.

28
00:01:56.647 --> 00:01:57.694
euphemism.

29
00:01:58.683 --> 00:01:59.684
doublespeak.

30
00:02:00.384 --> 00:02:04.009
doublespeak means language deliberately designed to hide meaning.

31
00:02:04.309 --> 00:02:07.876
'Collateral damage' is often criticised as doublespeak.

32
00:02:08.576 --> 00:02:11.414
Your turn. Say: doublespeak.

33
00:02:14.114 --> 00:02:15.092
doublespeak.

34
00:02:16.083 --> 00:02:17.746
political correctness.

35
00:02:18.446 --> 00:02:22.396
political correctness means avoiding language that might offend groups.

36
00:02:22.696 --> 00:02:25.659
Debates about political correctness are often heated.

37
00:02:26.359 --> 00:02:29.685
Your turn. Say: political correctness.

38
00:02:32.385 --> 00:02:33.920
political correctness.

39
00:02:34.917 --> 00:02:36.301
framing language.

40
00:02:37.001 --> 00:02:41.996
framing language means words chosen to present an issue in a particular light.

41
00:02:42.296 --> 00:02:46.188
'Tax relief' is framing language that presents tax as a burden.

42
00:02:46.888 --> 00:02:50.213
Your turn. Say: framing language.

43
00:02:52.913 --> 00:02:54.205
framing language.

44
00:02:55.217 --> 00:02:57.437
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:57.737 --> 00:03:01.698
Rithy, have you noticed how word choice shapes our view of events?

46
00:03:02.983 --> 00:03:09.235
Constantly. The description of a protest as a 'riot' implies violence and disorder.

47
00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:15.794
Exactly. That's a loaded term. And corporate euphemism is everywhere.

48
00:03:17.083 --> 00:03:20.163
'Workforce optimisation' instead of mass dismissal.

49
00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:25.725
The concealment of harsh realities through doublespeak has a long history.

50
00:03:27.017 --> 00:03:31.689
And accent can matter too. Mastery of a prestige dialect opens doors.

51
00:03:32.983 --> 00:03:38.201
Some call that the code of power. Its acquisition is rarely taught explicitly.

52
00:03:39.517 --> 00:03:43.269
Which makes awareness of framing language a form of empowerment.

53
00:03:44.583 --> 00:03:48.754
Grammar focus: Nominal style and lexical density.

54
00:03:49.054 --> 00:03:53.399
C2 academic style is lexically dense, built on noun phrases.

55
00:03:53.699 --> 00:04:00.983
The description of a protest as a riot implies violence. Mastery of a prestige dialect opens doors.

56
00:04:01.283 --> 00:04:03.446
Balance density with clarity.

57
00:04:03.746 --> 00:04:10.343
High lexical density packs ideas into noun phrases; balance it with clarity so that readers can follow.

58
00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:14.107
Now read and listen. Words That Rule.

59
00:04:14.407 --> 00:04:20.087
Language is not merely a tool for describing reality; it actively shapes our perception of it.

60
00:04:21.383 --> 00:04:26.123
The selection of a single loaded term can alter public sympathy for an entire group.

61
00:04:27.417 --> 00:04:33.840
Governments and corporations alike rely on euphemism to soften unwelcome news.

62
00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:40.389
George Orwell warned that political language is designed to make lies sound truthful.

63
00:04:41.683 --> 00:04:48.559
At a global level, the dominance of English has been described by some scholars as linguistic imperialism.

64
00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:55.762
Within societies, the privileging of a prestige dialect can reinforce social hierarchies.

65
00:04:57.050 --> 00:05:03.020
Critical awareness of framing language therefore becomes a form of intellectual self-defence.

66
00:05:04.317 --> 00:05:10.078
To understand power, one must first understand the words through which it is exercised.

67
00:05:11.383 --> 00:05:13.896
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:14.196 --> 00:05:17.403
A mild word used instead of a harsh one is a blank.

69
00:05:17.703 --> 00:05:22.982
A, euphemism. B, loaded term. C, code of power.

70
00:05:26.983 --> 00:05:29.145
The answer is A: euphemism.

71
00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:35.787
'They described the event as a riot.' → 'The blank of the event as a riot...'

72
00:05:36.087 --> 00:05:40.890
A, describe. B, description. C, describing.

73
00:05:44.883 --> 00:05:47.324
The answer is B: description.

74
00:05:48.817 --> 00:05:51.246
Which phrase is most lexically dense?

75
00:05:51.546 --> 00:05:57.962
A, because they hid the truth. B, the concealment of the truth. C, they were hiding it.

76
00:06:01.950 --> 00:06:05.285
The answer is B: the concealment of the truth.

77
00:06:06.783 --> 00:06:10.281
Language deliberately designed to hide meaning is blank.

78
00:06:10.581 --> 00:06:15.929
A, doublespeak. B, prestige dialect. C, framing language.

79
00:06:19.917 --> 00:06:22.926
The answer is A: doublespeak.

80
00:06:24.417 --> 00:06:28.042
According to the text, who warned about political language?

81
00:06:28.342 --> 00:06:32.274
A, Weber. B, Orwell. C, Descartes.

82
00:06:36.283 --> 00:06:38.515
The answer is B: Orwell.

83
00:06:40.017 --> 00:06:46.013
Now it's your turn. Part one: How does language reinforce or challenge power in society?

84
00:06:51.313 --> 00:06:57.538
Part two: Use nominal style: analyse two examples and propose one response.

85
00:07:03.838 --> 00:07:16.208
The use of euphemism by institutions and the privileging of urban accents both reinforce existing power. A meaningful response would be the explicit teaching of critical language awareness in schools.

86
00:07:16.508 --> 00:07:18.902
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:19.917 --> 00:07:25.646
Great work today! You learned eight new words and nominal style and lexical density.

88
00:07:25.946 --> 00:07:29.527
Today's value is respect. Thank the people who help us.

89
00:07:29.827 --> 00:07:32.142
See you in Unit four. Goodbye!

90
00:07:32.442 --> 00:07:35.780
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

